    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d519">

                <pb facs="0587" n="569" sameAs="frus:frus1946v11/pg569"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">825.34/4–1246: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Chile</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Boioers</hi>) <hi
                        rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left"><hi rendition="#small-caps">secret</hi></seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Santiago</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1946-04-12T16:00:00-04:00">April 12, 1946—4
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received 6:30 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>393. For Byrnes or Acheson and Braden. Very confidentially Admiral
                        Mayfield’s<anchor n="31" xml:id="d519-f1"/> Chilean aide has told him that
                    the British have heard of our proposition re offers to Chilean Navy and has
                    asked the Chilean Govt to be in no hurry since at a lower cost England will let
                    them have transports, tankers, etc at a much lower cost than we will ask and
                    that the British may also make available some cruisers. Because of the
                    confidential nature of this disclosure by the Chilean aide of Mayfield’s it is a
                    delicate matter to inquire into further but shall transmit promptly any more
                        info we may get through our Naval Attaché
                    or Mission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Bowers</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d519-f1" n="31" xml:id="d519-f1-content">
                <p>Adm. Irving H. Mayfield, Chief of the United States Naval Mission to Chile.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
